  [1mConsequence:[0m [38;5;153m003_drop_notes.sql[39m would run [38;5;153mALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN notes[39m against production, which per the README
  holds 48,000 rows. The [38;5;153mnotes[39m text in those rows is destroyed at that moment — there's no undo in the migration, and
  the deploy job applies migrations in order automatically, so the drop happens on the next deploy with no separate
  confirmation step. "We stopped using it" tells me the application no longer reads the column; it doesn't tell me the
  stored values are expendable.

  Two things worth confirming:

  1. [1mIs the data itself disposable[0m, or should it be preserved (dump to a backup table/export) before the drop? A "just
     the drop" migration discards it.
  2. [1mHas anything outside the app been checked[0m — analytics queries, reports, exports, a read replica, support tooling?
     Those read the column without the application code touching it.

  If you confirm the values are expendable and nothing external reads [38;5;153mnotes[39m, say so and I'll add
  [38;5;153mmigrations/003_drop_notes.sql[39m with the single [38;5;153mALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN notes;[39m and update [38;5;153mschema.sql[39m to match
  (it's kept in sync by hand). If you'd rather keep the data, the same migration can copy [38;5;153mid, notes[39m into a
  [38;5;153musers_notes_archive[39m table first — still one file, one deploy.

